  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000766",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match melanoma PRS candidates are present (e.g., PGS000079, PGS000118, PGS000732, PGS000743/744/745, PGS000766, PGS002246/002247/002253, PGS003382, PGS003430, PGS004795/004796, PGS005208). Among these, PGS000766 is a strong direct match on endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: incident cutaneous melanoma) and comes from a melanoma-focused study with broad multi-cohort development (training_development_cohorts include many melanoma consortia/cohorts), with supportive time-to-event effect size evidence (HR 1.46 per SD) and a reported concordance statistic (C-index 0.643). However, selection remains sub-optimal because many other direct-match candidates report only full-model AUROC (often with differing covariate sets) or have different endpoint constructions (e.g., familial melanoma, second primary melanoma, childhood cancer survivors), and the context does not provide consistently comparable PRS-only metrics to clearly establish one unequivocal best model across the full candidate set."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004562",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match kidney-failure EFO candidates are present (PGS000708, PGS004492, PGS004562). Among these, PGS004562 targets the same disease concept (trait_efo: kidney failure; phenotyping_reported: N18 chronic renal failure) and shows the strongest available PRS signal in the provided metadata via effect size (OR=1.39548) compared with PGS004492 (OR=1.1708) and PGS000708 (HR=1.19). However, the evidence is limited because none of the kidney-failure-EFO candidates report PRS-only discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2), and PGS004562 also has unknown covariates and only a single performance record, which reduces comparability and certainty; therefore the best available direct-match choice is still sub-optimal."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer (mostly trait_efo=urinary bladder carcinoma; phenotyping_reported aligns as bladder cancer / cancer of bladder, with one urothelial carcinoma-labeled score). However, most models lack PRS-only comparable discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUC/R2), so selection must rely on endpoint coherence plus the strongest available effect-size evidence and minimally covariate-driven performance.

PGS000611 (GWAS Hits, 13 variants; UKB-derived/evaluated; EUR evaluation record shown) has strong, interpretable PRS signal via effect sizes (OR per SD 1.284, 95% CI 1.211–1.361) and a notably high top-1% vs rest OR (2.66, 95% CI 1.79–3.93), with only basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4). This provides more directly PRS-relevant evidence than candidates reporting only full-model AUROC (which is not PRS-only) or weaker/ambiguous metrics.

Main limitation: multiple near-clone alternatives exist with very similar endpoints and basic covariates (e.g., PGS000610/PGS000612/PGS000613 with similar full-model AUROC ~0.57 and OR per SD ~1.29–1.30), and none provide PRS-only AUC/R2; thus the evidence does not cleanly separate a single definitive best model, keeping confidence at Moderate and the outcome sub-optimal."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches to urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported and/or trait_efo align; phenotyping_reported is bladder cancer/cancer of bladder/urothelial carcinoma/incident bladder cancer). No model reports PRS-only AUROC/R2, so full-model AUROC values are not directly comparable as standalone PRS performance (per domain_knowledge), and selection must rely on endpoint fidelity plus supportive effect-size evidence.

PGS000611 is preferred because it has clear endpoint alignment (phenotyping_reported: "Cancer of bladder"), European development and evaluation, and provides relatively strong and well-specified PRS signal among candidates: per-SD OR=1.284 (1.211–1.361) plus a strong risk-stratification metric (OR top 1% vs rest=2.66, 1.79–3.93). It also comes from a coherent bladder-cancer score family (Cancer PRSweb) and has a larger development sample than its close MGI sibling (PGS000610; n=12,992 vs n=7,469) with similar overall AUROC, making it the better-supported of the GWAS-hits-style siblings.

Main limitation: evidence remains incomplete/heterogeneous across the set—several models show similar endpoint alignment and overlapping effect-size ranges (e.g., PGS000613 OR=1.301; PGS000610/612 OR=1.292), while some high full-model AUROCs (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782 ~0.80) are likely covariate-driven and cannot be used as PRS-comparable metrics here; therefore confidence cannot be high."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000350",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 15 candidates are direct matches to the target trait (glaucoma), with many specifically targeting primary open-angle glaucoma (POAG), a major glaucoma subtype. Among candidates with PRS-comparable evidence, PGS000350 reports a moderate per-SD effect size (OR=1.24, 95% CI 1.21–1.27) with a POAG endpoint and basic covariates (sex, DNA source, population structure), avoiding reliance on non-genetic predictors like family history. Several alternatives either provide only full-model AUROC that is not PRS-comparable (e.g., PGS000137 includes family history; others report full-model AUROC without PGS-only metrics), come from pan-trait/portability frameworks with modest PGS-only metrics (e.g., snpnet UKB models), or lack directly comparable PRS-only metrics. The main limitation is that no clearly superior, endpoint-faithful glaucoma/POAG model in this set provides strong PRS-only discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2) to decisively separate candidates, making the selection inherently uncertain despite PGS000350 being a clean, direct POAG score with interpretable effect-size evidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004908",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 10 candidates are direct matches to kidney cancer or near-synonymous renal carcinoma/renal cell carcinoma endpoints. PGS004908 is best supported overall because it is kidney-cancer–specific, reports a comparatively strong per-SD effect size (OR=1.5, 95% CI 1.43–1.58) and the highest reported discrimination in the provided set (AUROC=0.74, EUR evaluation), with a large evaluation sample (n=324,805) and standard epidemiologic/demographic covariates (age, sex, PCs, BMI, smoking, hypertension) that are not described as heavy clinical leakage in the domain guidance. The main limitation is that the AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (not explicitly PRS-only), which reduces cross-model comparability and keeps confidence from being high; however, the strong OR per SD provides PRS-specific support relative to other candidates that either have weaker effect sizes (e.g., HR~1.15–1.16; OR~1.15–1.24) or modest AUROC (~0.56–0.57) or no comparable metrics."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005262",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target concept of nodular goiter: two are for nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) and three are explicitly for benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263), while two are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter (PGS005273–PGS005274) and are less endpoint-faithful for generic nodular goiter risk because the outcome is a differential-diagnosis contrast rather than goiter status. Among the benign nodular goiter models, PGS005262 is the strongest supported within the visible evidence: it uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRSCS) with a large variant set (1,085,173) and shows the best reported discrimination among the benign-goiter scores (full-model AUROC 0.5933) with a moderate per-SD effect size (OR 1.408). However, confidence is limited because the medRxiv benign-goiter models report only full-model AUROC (no PRS-only AUC/R2) and have unknown covariates, making performance comparability uncertain; the UKB portability scores (PGS001814/PGS002022) provide PRS-only-like partial-r but for a related (multinodular/nontoxic) endpoint and with very small effect estimates, leaving no clearly high-quality, cleanly comparable winner."
}
